<span class="kicker">2026-08-11 / RIDGEWELL GUIDES</span><h1>How to Build a Paver Walkway and Patio Together</h1>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section article-section"><div class="wrap editorial-layout"><article class="prose blog-content"><p>Building a walkway and patio as one connected project is a different kind of challenge than building either one on its own. The individual construction steps, excavation, base, laying pavers, jointing, are largely the same. What actually makes or breaks the result is the transition between the two spaces, how the walkway hands off to the patio in material, pattern, and level, so the finished project reads as one cohesive design rather than two separate installations that happen to touch.</p>
<p>At Ridgewell Landscape and Design, walkway and patio combination projects are some of the most common builds we take on across the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and the surrounding communities, and the transition point is where we spend the most design attention. This guide covers how to plan and build a walkway and patio together, with a close look at getting that connection right, along with cost considerations for building both at once versus separately.</p>
<h2><b>Why the Transition Point Matters Most</b></h2>
<p>A walkway and a patio built independently, even with similar materials, often end up feeling like two different projects stitched together. A walkway and patio built as one connected system, planned together from the start, reads as a single, intentional outdoor space. The difference almost always comes down to how carefully the transition between them was handled, not the quality of either space on its own.</p>
<h2><b>Matching Pattern and Material Between Walkway and Patio</b></h2>
<h3><b>Using the Same Paver Throughout</b></h3>
<p>The simplest and most reliably cohesive approach is using the exact same paver product, color, and finish for both the walkway and patio. This removes any guesswork around whether two similar-but-different products will read as intentional or mismatched once installed side by side.</p>
<h3><b>Using a Complementary Paver for the Walkway</b></h3>
<p>Some designs benefit from a slightly different, but clearly complementary, paver for the walkway versus the patio, for example a smaller format paver for the walkway leading into a larger format paver field on the patio. This works well when there’s a deliberate design reason for the distinction, but it requires more careful color and tone matching than using an identical product throughout.</p>
<h3><b>Pattern Continuity Versus Pattern Change</b></h3>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Approach</b></td><td><b>Best For</b></td><td><b>Consideration</b></td></tr><tr><td>Same pattern throughout</td><td>Simple, cohesive look</td><td>Easiest to execute cleanly</td></tr><tr><td>Different pattern, same paver</td><td>Visual variety with cohesion</td><td>Needs a clear transition line</td></tr><tr><td>Different pattern, different paver</td><td>Distinct zones, bold design</td><td>Highest risk of feeling disconnected</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Running the same pattern, like herringbone, continuously from walkway into patio creates the most seamless transition. Changing pattern while keeping the same paver, for example running bond on the walkway shifting to herringbone on the patio, still reads as cohesive as long as the change happens at a clear, deliberate line, like where the walkway meets the patio’s edge, rather than an arbitrary point partway through.</p>
<h2><b>Designing the Transition Point</b></h2>
<h3><b>A Deliberate Border or Edge Line</b></h3>
<p>One of the cleanest ways to handle a transition, especially when pattern or paver size changes between the two spaces, is a deliberate border course, a row or two of pavers running perpendicular to both spaces, marking the transition clearly rather than letting two different patterns awkwardly meet at an undefined point.</p>
<h3><b>Widening Gradually Into the Patio</b></h3>
<p>Rather than a walkway meeting a patio at an abrupt width change, a gradual widening as the walkway approaches the patio creates a more natural visual flow. This works particularly well when the walkway is noticeably narrower than the patio, which is common since walkways typically run 3 to 5 feet wide while patios are often considerably wider.</p>
<h3><b>Managing Level Changes Between the Two Spaces</b></h3>
<p>If your site has any slope between where the walkway starts and where the patio sits, managing that elevation change smoothly is critical to the transition feeling intentional rather than awkward. This might mean a very gradual grade change across the walkway’s length, or in some cases a shallow step or two right at the transition point if the elevation change is more significant. Our guide on<a href="/blog/how-to-build-a-paver-walkway-on-a-slope/"> building a paver walkway on a slope</a> covers grading and stepped approaches that apply directly to managing this kind of transition.</p>

<h2>Turn Your dream landscape a reality</h2>

