<h2><b>What You’ll Need for a Combined Project</b></h2>
<p><b>Materials</b></p>
<ul><li>Pavers for both spaces, plus 5 to 10 percent extra for cuts and waste</li><li>Crushed gravel or road base, 4 to 6 inches deep depending on soil type</li><li>Landscape fabric</li><li>Coarse bedding sand</li><li>Polymeric sand for joints</li><li>Edge restraints and spikes</li></ul>
<p><b>Tools</b></p>
<ul><li>Shovel and spade</li><li>A<a href="/blog/choose-the-right-plate-compactor-size-for-a-patio-or-driveway/"> properly sized plate compactor</a></li><li>Rubber mallet</li><li>String line and stakes</li><li>4 foot level</li><li>Paver saw or angle grinder with a masonry blade</li><li>Push broom</li></ul>
<h2><b>Step by Step: Building a Connected Walkway and Patio</b></h2>
<h3><b>Step 1: Design Both Spaces Together From the Start</b></h3>
<p>Before any digging, lay out both the walkway and patio as one connected design, not two separate plans, paying close attention to how width, pattern, and material will meet at the transition point.</p>
<h3><b>Step 2: Mark the Full Layout</b></h3>
<p>Use stakes and string to mark both the walkway path and patio footprint together, so you can see the full project in real dimensions and confirm the transition feels right before excavation begins.</p>
<h3><b>Step 3: Excavate Both Spaces to Consistent Depth</b></h3>
<p>Dig out both areas accounting for base, sand, and paver thickness combined, keeping depth consistent across the transition point so the finished surfaces meet at a smooth, level line rather than a visible seam.</p>
<h3><b>Step 4: Build and Compact One Continuous Base</b></h3>
<p>Add crushed gravel in 2 inch layers across both spaces, compacting thoroughly after each layer. Treating the base as one continuous system rather than two separate ones helps prevent uneven settling right at the transition point down the line.</p>
<h3><b>Step 5: Add Landscape Fabric and Bedding Sand</b></h3>
<p>Roll landscape fabric over the compacted base, then add and screed about 1 inch of coarse sand across both spaces, maintaining a consistent, level plane through the transition.</p>
<h3><b>Step 6: Lay Pavers Starting From the Transition Point</b></h3>
<p>Rather than building each space independently and hoping they meet cleanly, many installers start laying pavers from the transition point outward in both directions, since this ensures the connection itself is precise before extending the pattern into either space.</p>
<h3><b>Step 7: Cut Border Pavers Last</b></h3>
<p>Once the main fields of both spaces are laid, measure and cut border pieces individually to fit the remaining edge gaps around the full perimeter.</p>
<h3><b>Step 8: Install Edge Restraints Around the Full Perimeter</b></h3>
<p>Secure edge restraints around the entire combined footprint, walkway and patio together, anchoring with spikes to lock the whole system in place.</p>
<h3><b>Step 9: Fill Joints With Polymeric Sand</b></h3>
<p>Sweep polymeric sand into the joints across both spaces, remove excess sand from the surface, and mist to activate.</p>
<h3><b>Step 10: Final Compaction</b></h3>
<p>Run the plate compactor over the entire finished project one final time, checking closely at the transition point for any inconsistency in level or joint spacing.</p>
<h2><b>Is It Cheaper to Build Both Together Versus Separately</b></h2>
<p>Building a walkway and patio at the same time generally costs less overall than building them as two separate projects at different points in time, for a few concrete reasons.</p>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Factor</b></td><td><b>Combined Project</b></td><td><b>Separate Projects</b></td></tr><tr><td>Mobilization and site setup</td><td>One time cost</td><td>Paid twice</td></tr><tr><td>Material ordering</td><td>Bulk pricing on larger combined order</td><td>Smaller individual orders</td></tr><tr><td>Labor efficiency</td><td>Continuous work, no re-setup</td><td>Crew re-establishes site each time</td></tr><tr><td>Base work</td><td>One continuous, connected base</td><td>Separate bases, harder to match perfectly</td></tr><tr><td>Design cohesion</td><td>Planned together from the start</td><td>Requires matching an existing installation later</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Beyond direct cost savings, building both together also removes the challenge of matching a new installation to an existing one months or years later, when the original paver product may no longer be available in the same color or batch. If you know a patio is in your future plans even if you’re only building the walkway now, it’s worth discussing that intention with your contractor upfront, since some site and base planning decisions made during the walkway phase can make a future patio addition easier to tie in cleanly.</p>
<h2><b>Common Mistakes When Combining a Walkway and Patio Project</b></h2>
<ul><li>Designing the walkway and patio as separate projects rather than one connected system from the start</li><li>Choosing similar-but-not-identical pavers, resulting in a subtle but noticeable mismatch</li><li>Ignoring elevation changes between the two spaces, leading to an awkward transition</li><li>Building inconsistent base depth between the walkway and patio, risking uneven settling at the connection point</li><li>Not planning ahead for a future patio addition when only building the walkway initially</li></ul>
<h2><b>Why Planning Both Spaces Together Pays Off</b></h2>
<p>A walkway and patio built as one thoughtfully connected project consistently reads as more finished and intentional than the same two spaces built independently, even when using similar materials. The transition point, matched pattern, and continuous base work are the details that separate a combined outdoor living space from what feels like two unrelated hardscape projects placed next to each other.</p>
<p>With over 15 years of designing and building combined walkway and patio projects across the Denver area, our team at Ridgewell Landscape and Design pays close attention to exactly this kind of connection point, since it’s often what determines whether a finished project feels cohesive or disjointed. Explore our<a href="/services/paver-patios/"> paver patio services</a> to see examples of combined walkway and patio designs.</p>

<h2><b>Frequently Asked Questions</b></h2>
<h3><b>Should a walkway and patio use the exact same paver?</b></h3>
<p>Using the exact same paver is the simplest way to ensure a cohesive look, though a complementary paver can work well when there’s a deliberate design reason and careful color matching.</p>
<h3><b>Is it cheaper to build a walkway and patio at the same time?</b></h3>
<p>Generally yes, due to shared mobilization costs, bulk material pricing, more efficient labor, and a continuous base system, compared to building them as separate projects at different times.</p>
<h3><b>How do you handle a slope between the walkway and patio?</b></h3>
<p>Options include a gradual grade change across the walkway’s length or a shallow step at the transition point if the elevation change is more significant, similar to techniques used for sloped walkways generally.</p>
<h3><b>Can I build the walkway now and add a matching patio later?</b></h3>
<p>Yes, though it’s worth discussing that future plan with your contractor during the walkway phase, since certain site and base decisions can make a later patio addition easier to tie in cleanly.</p>
<h3><b>What’s the best way to handle the transition if the patio and walkway use different patterns?</b></h3>
<p>A deliberate border course, a row or two of pavers running perpendicular to both spaces, creates a clear, intentional transition line rather than letting two patterns meet at an undefined point.</p>

<h2><b>Ready to Build a Connected Walkway and Patio</b></h2>
<p>A walkway and patio built together as one thoughtful design consistently outperforms two separately built spaces, both in how it looks and in overall project cost.</p>
